{"established":["The proposed indication is glioma (patient-derived lines), a setting with a genuinely severe unmet need — glioblastoma median survival remains roughly 15 months on maximal standard of care (resection, radiotherapy, temozolomide, TTFields), and no systemic agent has moved that bar meaningfully in two decades.","Standard of care in glioma is stable enough that it is not going to obsolete a novel target program: temozolomide has been the backbone since 2005, and recent additions (TTFields, vorasidenib in IDH-mutant low-grade glioma) are additive rather than displacing, so the gap this program would address is unlikely to close on its own.","Patient-derived lines rather than serially passaged commercial lines is the appropriate preclinical substrate for this indication, which is the right instinct for a disease where standard lines poorly reflect tumor biology."],"concerns":["The hub's framing question — whether preclinical target ranking is a real decision point in glioma — has a specific answer that cuts against a target-agnostic ranking claim: glioma drug development is not gated on target ranking, it is gated on blood-brain-barrier penetration and on the near-universal failure of systemically active agents to reproduce in-brain effects. A 60% proliferation drop in vitro carries almost no predictive weight in this indication for that reason, and no CNS exposure consideration has been raised at all.","The indication is described only as 'glioma,' which is not a development-ready population. Glioma spans IDH-mutant low-grade tumors (now served by vorasidenib, median PFS extended substantially), IDH-wildtype GBM, and pediatric H3K27M-altered diffuse midline glioma — these have different biology, different trial designs, and different regulatory paths. No molecular slice has been proposed, and nothing in the record indicates whether the target's dependency tracks with any subgroup.","No biomarker links the target to a selectable patient population, so the current path is an all-comers recurrent GBM trial — historically the highest-attrition setting in oncology, with no validated interim surrogate the FDA accepts for accelerated approval. Response rate in GBM is a weak endpoint; PFS is contested because of pseudoprogression; the realistic path is an overall-survival readout.","Addressable population is small for an unstratified program: GBM incidence is roughly 3 per 100,000, around 12–13,000 new US cases annually, and the recurrent population eligible for a first-in-human trial is a fraction of that. If the mechanism turns out to be subgroup-restricted, the number could fall to a level that supports orphan designation but strains a commercial return without pricing assumptions no one has made yet.","The PI has not looked at the competitive landscape by his own admission, which in glioma matters because the field is crowded with target-based programs at the same stage of maturity, and the differentiator is almost never target novelty."],"questions_to_ask":["When you say glioma — which glioma? Is this IDH-wildtype GBM, or does the dependency also show up in IDH-mutant or H3K27M lines? Because those are three different drug programs with three different trial designs, and vorasidenib has already changed what the bar looks like in the IDH-mutant setting.","Do you see any pattern in which of your patient-derived lines respond and which don't — MGMT status, IDH, EGFR amplification, anything? If there's a molecular feature that separates responders from non-responders, that's the difference between an all-comers recurrent GBM survival trial and a biomarker-selected trial you could actually power.","Has anyone on the team thought about brain penetration for this target class? Not the chemistry — I mean whether the biology requires sustained intratumoral target engagement, and whether you'd expect a systemically dosed molecule to achieve it, because that's where most glioma target programs die rather than at the target-validation step.","If this ever reached the clinic, what would the registrational endpoint be? In recurrent GBM there's no surrogate the agency has accepted for accelerated approval, so I'd want to know whether you're picturing an overall-survival trial and roughly what population size you think that takes.","Have you checked whether anyone else has published this target as a glioma dependency — a DepMap query or a look at the published CRISPR screens in glioma lines would tell us in an afternoon whether preclinical ranking here is genuinely open ground or already contested."],"verdict_signal":"caution","confidence":"moderate"}

