{"established":["Recurrent glioblastoma is a genuine high unmet need: after failure of radiation plus temozolomide, lomustine monotherapy delivers median OS in the 7-9 month range with response rates in the single digits, and no agent has shown a survival benefit in a randomised phase III since bevacizumab (PFS only, no OS). The PI's characterisation of 'outcomes are poor' is correct and not an overstatement.","There is no biomarker-selected standard of care in recurrent GBM outside of the rare BRAF V600E and NTRK-fusion slices, so a new mechanism does not have to displace an entrenched targeted agent — the competitive bar at the level of clinical benefit is low.","Incidence supports a trial: roughly 12,000-13,000 new GBM diagnoses per year in the US, and essentially all recur, so the recurrent population is a few thousand accruable patients per year across cooperative groups and academic centres. That is enough for a randomised trial against lomustine, and GBM carries orphan designation in both US and EU."],"concerns":["The question the hub asked is the sharpest one here, and the answer is unfavourable: recurrent GBM development is not gated on preclinical target ranking. It is gated on CNS exposure. A decade of programmes with strong target biology — EGFR, PI3K, CDK4/6, PARP — have failed in GBM not because the target was wrongly ranked but because free drug did not reach tumour at pharmacologically active concentrations, particularly in the infiltrating margin behind an intact blood-brain barrier. A ranked target list does not move a GBM developer; a brain-penetrant chemical series does. The PI has an 800 nM phenol with no PK, which is the worst possible starting point for a CNS indication (phenols are efflux and glucuronidation liabilities).","No indication rationale has been offered at all. Nothing in the account connects NSMCE3 to glioma biology — not expression in glioma versus normal brain, not a dependency signal in glioma lines, not a genetic or transcriptional subgroup. The explants are described only as 'two primary human explants' with no statement that they were glioma. If the target selection was mechanism-first and the indication chosen because it is an obvious unmet need, that is indication-by-desperation, and reviewers and funders will read it that way.","NSMCE3 sits in the SMC5/6 complex — DNA damage repair and replication-fork biology. That mechanism class points naturally to a synthetic-lethal or biomarker-defined framing (homologous recombination status, ATRX loss, MGMT status, replication stress markers) rather than all-comers recurrent GBM. An all-comers design in a mechanism whose effect size will be subgroup-concentrated is the design most likely to produce a null result that kills the programme without informing it.","Genome-instability targets in normal proliferating tissue carry a real therapeutic-index problem — myelosuppression in particular. In recurrent GBM the patients have already had temozolomide and often lomustine, both marrow-toxic, so the population is precisely the one least able to tolerate an additional DNA-repair-directed agent. No tox data exists to speak to this.","There is no accelerated-approval path in recurrent GBM. FDA has not accepted ORR or PFS as a surrogate for full approval in this disease (the bevacizumab accelerated approval was subsequently the subject of a confirmatory-trial dispute), so the realistic path is an OS-powered randomised trial against lomustine — long, expensive, high-attrition, with no early clean readout to de-risk the next financing round.","Standard-of-care drift in recurrent GBM is modest but not zero: TTFields is already in the frontline label and being pushed into recurrence, and vorasidenib's approval has reset the IDH-mutant low-grade glioma landscape and is pulling investigator attention and trial slots. More importantly, several bispecific and CAR-T programmes are accruing in recurrent GBM. None of these obsolete a new mechanism, but they compete for the same limited accrual pool."],"questions_to_ask":["Why glioblastoma? Walk me through what connects NSMCE3 specifically to glioma — expression in tumour versus normal brain, dependency in glioma lines, anything — because right now it reads as if the indication was chosen because the unmet need is obvious rather than because the biology points there.","Were the two human explants glioma, or another tissue? And if glioma, were they primary or recurrent, and do you know their MGMT and IDH status?","NSMCE3 is SMC5/6 — replication-fork and DNA-repair biology. Is there a subgroup where you would expect the effect to be much larger: HR-deficient, ATRX-null, MGMT-methylated, high replication stress? If you had to pick one biomarker-defined slice to run the first trial in, which would it be and what is the evidence for it?","Have you or anyone thought about brain penetration for this chemical series? In recurrent GBM the graveyard is full of correct targets whose drugs never reached the infiltrating margin — what makes you think NSMCE3 is druggable behind an intact BBB?","Your patients will have already had temozolomide and often lomustine. If you are inhibiting a genome-stability complex on top of that, what do you expect to happen to their marrow, and is there any tissue-selectivity argument for why normal proliferating cells are spared?","If the answer to the indication question is that GBM is not actually the best fit, what would you pick instead — is there a tumour type or a genetic background where NSMCE3 loss is a known dependency?"],"verdict_signal":"blocking","confidence":"moderate"}

